{"id":"h038","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"If the vapor pressure of CCl_4 (carbon tetrachloride) is .132 atm at 23°C and .526 atm at 58°C, what is the ∆H' in this temperature range?\nIs the correct answer \"37,000 (J / mole)\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is not 37,000 J/mol; the data give about 32,000 J/mol.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The two-point Clausius-Clapeyron equation is the appropriate method for finding ΔH from these vapor pressures.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Treating ΔH_vap as constant is a reasonable approximation over the modest 23–58°C range.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Neglecting liquid molar volume and treating the vapor as ideal are standard approximations that are acceptable here.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Two data points give only a rough estimate that is sensitive to rounding in the pressures.","confidence":0.6,"lean":0.0,"theta":0.9425,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The calculation yields ln(P2/P1) of about 1.382 and a 1/T difference of about -3.567e-4 per K.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The computed ΔH is about 3.2e4 J/mol.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The computed value agrees with CCl4's known enthalpy of vaporization near room temperature (~30–32 kJ/mol).","confidence":0.75,"lean":-1,"theta":1.1781,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The value 37,000 J/mol does not follow from these data under the stated assumptions.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relat